"Extends a proposed plan with potential failures and countermeasures so that teams can prepare before execution.\n\nUse when: Stress-test a new plan before committing resources. Identify feasible responses to foreseeable operational disruptions.\n\nAvoid when: The plan is too undefined to identify meaningful stages and failure points.\n\nVisual grammar: A task hierarchy establishes the proposed plan. Failure branches state what could go wrong at specific steps. Countermeasure branches show possible responses and their feasibility.\n\nBuild: Break the proposed plan into practical stages or tasks. Ask what could prevent each stage from succeeding. Generate countermeasures and mark feasible actions, owners and preparation needs.\n\nCheck: Each failure is linked to a specific plan element. Countermeasures address the named failure mechanism. Rejected measures are distinguished from chosen preparations.\n\nPitfalls: Listing generic worries detached from the actual plan. Adding a countermeasure without checking whether it can be executed in time.\n\nExample: An outdoor event plan adds rain, power loss and supplier delays beneath relevant tasks, with shelter, backup power and substitute arrangements.\n\nAlternatives: Risk matrix, Bow-tie diagram."
